{"id": "src-ch05-sec02-tail-p402-b008", "batch_id": "ch05-sec02-tail", "pdf_page": 402, "printed_page": 391, "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "environment_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "number": null, "source_text": "3 The steady Navier–Stokes equations", "formula_ids": [], "label": null, "references": [], "footnote_ids": [], "figure_ids": [], "translation_status": "not-translated", "confidence": 0.68, "review_status": "needs-review"}
{"id": "src-ch05-sec02-tail-p402-b009", "batch_id": "ch05-sec02-tail", "pdf_page": 402, "printed_page": 391, "block_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "environment_type": "ordinary-paragraph", "number": null, "source_text": "In this section, we deal with steady solutions for the Navier–Stokes equations.\n       We first establish the existence of at least one solution for space dimension\n       2 and 3. We consider homogeneous and non-homogenous Dirichlet boundary\n       conditions. Then we prove the uniqueness of such solution for small data and\n       we finally establish asymptotic stability properties of such solutions.", "formula_ids": [], "label": null, "references": [], "footnote_ids": [], "figure_ids": [], "translation_status": "not-translated", "confidence": 0.68, "review_status": "needs-review"}
